# Twilight Forest -- block descriptions.
#
# BTA scans assets/<namespace>/lang/en_US/ for any *.lang and loads each into ONE Properties object,
# so which file a key lives in does not matter -- but a key defined in two files does, because the
# load order is directory-enumeration order and the winner is whichever happens to be read last.
#
# ⚠️ SO THIS FILE CONTAINS NO KEY THAT twilightforest.lang ALREADY HAS. Every block and item NAME is
# defined there and stays there. What is here is the `.desc` half, which nothing had before: BTA's own
# tile.lang carries 745 of them, so it is an established key and not an invention.
#
# The names are Benimatic's. The descriptions are not -- upstream wrote none, because Twilight Forest
# 1.7.1 predates the guidebook entirely. They are this port's, and they are written to say what a
# block DOES rather than what it looks like, because in this mod those are usually different.

# --- Mazestone --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The whole point of the block is that its appearance is a lie -- and the lie holds through the drop
# as well, since it yields whatever the block it imitates would have. So the only tell is the cost,
# and that is what the description says. See BlockLogicTFMazestone: hardness 20, and 16 durability
# off a pickaxe per block.
tile.twilightforest.mazestone.desc=Passes for ordinary stone, and breaks like it. Ruins a pickaxe sixteen times faster.
tile.twilightforest.mazestone.cobble.desc=Passes for ordinary cobblestone, and breaks like it. Ruins a pickaxe sixteen times faster.
tile.twilightforest.mazestone.mossy.desc=Passes for ordinary mossy cobblestone, and breaks like it. Ruins a pickaxe sixteen times faster.

# --- Hedge ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tile.twilightforest.hedge.desc=A wall of thorns. It bites anything that touches it, except spiders.

# --- The dark and enchanted forests -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The darkwood canopy's description leads with the thing a player will actually notice -- that it is
# solid, unlike every other leaf -- because that is what a dark forest feels like from underneath.
# It deliberately does NOT mention biting, since it does not, and its sibling three lines up does.
tile.twilightforest.log.darkwood.desc=Black-barked wood from the dark forest.
tile.twilightforest.leaves.darkwood.desc=A solid canopy, not a loose one. It shuts out the sky, and unlike a hedge it is harmless.
tile.twilightforest.roots.desc=Grows under a dark forest tree rather than on top of one. Breaks into sticks.
# The description leads with "hangs" because that is the thing a player will get wrong: it is the only
# plant in the game that needs a block ABOVE it, and one placed hopefully on a cave floor vanishes on
# the next block update. Soil rather than stone is the second surprise and is upstream's own rule --
# see BlockLogicTFTorchberries.canPlaceRootBelow.
tile.twilightforest.torchberries.desc=Hangs from soil, never stone, and glows half as bright as a torch. Found where a cave has cut in under the forest floor.
# Same hanging rule as the torchberry above, so the description leads with the SECOND surprise
# instead: bare hands get nothing. That is upstream's -- BlockTFPlant.getBlockDropped is an empty
# case for metadata 14 and only its shear path yields the block.
tile.twilightforest.root_strands.desc=Hangs from soil or from the roots above it, in long chains. Bare hands tear it apart; shears take it whole.
tile.twilightforest.leaves.rainbow.desc=No two are the same colour. The shade depends on where the leaf grew, not on the season or the soil.

# --- The four magic trees ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These descriptions have a harder job than the rest of the file, because what these blocks DO is the
# one thing this port does not carry: the powers live in a fifth block per species -- the Timewood
# Clock, the Heart of Transformation, the Minewood Core, the Sortingwood Engine -- and upstream marks
# all four [WIP] in its own lang file. So each line says what the wood is FOR without promising a
# behaviour the player will not find, and the leaf lines describe the one thing that is genuinely
# here and genuinely worth looking at, which is the colour.
tile.twilightforest.log.timewood.desc=Cut from a tree that keeps its own hours.
tile.twilightforest.log.transwood.desc=Pale wood from a tree that changes what stands near it.
tile.twilightforest.log.minewood.desc=Dense wood from a tree that grows over buried stone.
tile.twilightforest.log.sortingwood.desc=Wood from a tree that likes things in their proper place.
tile.twilightforest.leaves.timewood.desc=Green to burnt orange, and which one depends entirely on where the leaf is.
tile.twilightforest.leaves.transformation.desc=Cyan fading to slate in bands up the tree, rather than across the forest.
tile.twilightforest.leaves.miners.desc=Two yellows so close together that the whole crown seems to glow.
tile.twilightforest.leaves.sorting.desc=Cycles from near-black to bright yellow-green four times as fast as Timewood does.

# The saplings. These carry the honest version of what upstream compresses into a [WIP] tag -- see
# the note in twilightforest.lang for why the tag itself is not shipped. Each line says what the tree
# is and, where it matters, that its heart is missing; a player who plants one and waits for a clock
# to appear should be told beforehand rather than left to wonder.
#
# The leaf drop is worth mentioning on the sapling rather than on the leaves, because it is the
# sapling the player is looking for when they want a second tree.
tile.twilightforest.sapling.timewood.desc=Grows a squat Timewood tree with a ladder up the middle. Its clock is not built yet.
tile.twilightforest.sapling.transformation.desc=Grows a low, wide Transwood canopy. Its heart is not built yet.
tile.twilightforest.sapling.miners.desc=Grows a narrow Minewood fan, roots and all. Its core is not built yet.
tile.twilightforest.sapling.sorting.desc=Grows the smallest tree in the forest, four blocks of Sortingwood. Its engine is not built yet.

# --- The critters -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tile.twilightforest.firefly.desc=A living light. Clings to walls, floors and ceilings, and drifts sparks when you are near.
tile.twilightforest.cicada.desc=Clings to bark and leaves, and sings.

# --- Boss spawner -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tile.twilightforest.boss_spawner.desc=Something is waiting. It fires once, from fifty blocks away.

# Giant mushrooms. Vanilla's blocks 99/100, which BTA does not have and this port supplies -- the
# canopy mushroom's cap and stalk. The stalk is data 10 on the brown block, so it needs no name of
# its own: a player only ever holds the cap form.
tile.twilightforest.mushroom_giant.brown.name=Giant Brown Mushroom
tile.twilightforest.mushroom_giant.red.name=Giant Red Mushroom

# --- The Ur-Ghast's dark tower ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Towerwood. The description that matters is the asymmetry: hardness 40 against a blast resistance of
# 10, so a pickaxe is hopeless and TNT is not. A player who reads only one of these lines should come
# away knowing not to try mining the tower.
tile.twilightforest.tower_wood.desc=The dark tower's timber. Twice as hard as obsidian to mine, and no tougher than stone against an explosion.
tile.twilightforest.tower_wood.encased.desc=The tower's framing timber. Same stuff, bound at the edges.
tile.twilightforest.tower_wood.cracked.desc=Towerwood that has started to give.
tile.twilightforest.tower_wood.mossy.desc=Towerwood the forest has begun to take back.
# ⚠️ Says outright that it drops nothing. The block is a trap -- it is the only towerwood you can break
# by hand, and breaking it costs you the block and gains you a termite -- and a trap the player is
# warned about is a mechanic rather than an annoyance.
tile.twilightforest.tower_wood.infested.desc=Soft, and hollow. It yields nothing, and something lives in it.

# The devices, keyed by metadata. Only the six the creative menu offers are described; the transient
# "active" states are on screen for a fifth of a second and nobody reads a tooltip for them.
tile.twilightforest.tower_device.0.desc=Touch it or power it and it goes, taking its neighbours with it -- then comes back four seconds later.
tile.twilightforest.tower_device.2.desc=Touch it or power it and it goes for good, taking its neighbours with it. Cannot be mined.
tile.twilightforest.tower_device.4.desc=Holds every vanishing block within two of it. A tower key opens it.
tile.twilightforest.tower_device.5.desc=A lock that has been opened. It vanishes like any other now.
tile.twilightforest.tower_device.6.desc=Power it and it lays a bridge of tower blocks in whatever direction the nearest player is looking.
tile.twilightforest.tower_device.9.desc=Remembers the nine blocks around it in every direction and undoes whatever you build there.
# The two [WIP] pairs. Upstream gives them a name, a texture and nothing else, and saying so is better
# than letting a player hunt for a mechanism that was never written.
tile.twilightforest.tower_device.10.desc=Part of the Ur-Ghast's tower. It does nothing yet.
tile.twilightforest.tower_device.12.desc=Part of the Ur-Ghast's tower. It does nothing yet.
